{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>If he comes in his body, etc.</b> Our Sages of blessed memory in Kiddushin (20a) said: single he enters, single he leaves — from here we learn that the master is not permitted to give him a Canaanite maidservant unless he is married. And it appears that the superfluity of the verse was difficult for them: why was it necessary to say this? In any case, if the intent is to say that specifically when he enters בְּגַפּוֹ (in his body) he should leave בְּגַפּוֹ, but when he enters with his wife he should not leave בְּגַפּוֹ — this is self-evident, for who brought her in that it should be necessary to say so? And you will find that our Sages of blessed memory (Kiddushin 22a) examined the statement ״וְיָצְאָה אִשְׁתּוֹ״ (and his wife shall leave) and concluded that it should not have been necessary to state it, and they interpreted it to obligate the master to provide food for his wife and children. And if the verse concerns a maidservant that his master gives him, it is not necessary, for he says immediately afterward: ״הָאִשָּׁה וִילָדֶיהָ תִּהְיֶה לַאדֹנֶיהָ וְהוּא יֵצֵא בְגַפּוֹ״. Rather, it surely comes for exposition, to say ״אִם בְּגַפּוֹ״ — meaning, without an Israelite wife, as is revealed at the end of its words: ״אִם בַּעַל אִשָּׁה״ etc. <b>בְּגַפּוֹ יֵצֵא</b> — meaning, single he shall leave, that is, he shall remain in the same quantity in which he was single until his departure. Or on this path, Scripture juxtaposes departure to entry: just as when he entered he entered truly single, without wife and children — both those who go out with him and those who remain in the master's house — so too at his departure. And if he had a wife but had no children, likewise his master does not give him a maidservant, concerning whom we apply the term בְּגַפּוֹ, and all the more so if he had children but had no wife." "<b>And I saw</b> in the Rav Rabbi Eliyahu Mizrachi that he was precise in noting that it should have said ״אִם בְּגַפּוֹ וְגוֹ׳ כֵּן יֵצֵא״ (if in his body, etc., so shall he leave). And his words do not appear correct, for on the contrary, if it had said thus it would have been heard more clearly that he may not give him a maidservant and he should remain as he was. And the truth is that the reason it did not say so is that there is reason to be concerned about other interpretations that may be heard from it, and the discerning one will understand." "And if you say: from where do our Sages of blessed memory (Kiddushin 20a) derive that they included the children as well — for although these matters are halakhah, one may say that if he enters with a wife but without children, I would apply to him ״בְּגַפּוֹ יֵצֵא״ — that his master may not give him a maidservant and he may have children from her, in which case his 'body' of departure would not match his 'body' of entry, for then he had no children and now he has children. And if at the time he entered he had a wife but no children and afterward he had children, he entered under a condition of permissibility. And perhaps it is for this that the Torah conditioned the matter on the wife, who must be present at the time of his entry into servitude, but the children even if they come afterward. And if he had a wife when his master acquired him and she died, it is implied that it is permitted, for the verse was particular only about the time of entry: ״בְּגַפּוֹ יָבֹא״. And were it not for their words of blessed memory, I would say that the verse removed this ruling from its statement ״אִם בַּעַל אִשָּׁה הוּא״ — his wife must be with him until the time of departure, and on this basis I permit you the following: if his master gives him a wife." "<b>If he is a husband of a woman, etc.</b> This is explained according to the statement of our Sages of blessed memory (Mekhilta Ahariti deRabbi Shimon) that the master is not obligated to provide food for the slave's wife, unless she be a wife who is fitting for him to remain with, and not a wife who is forbidden to him by negative prohibitions. Now, regarding the giving of a Canaanite maidservant, it is implied from the Talmud and cited in the words of the Rambam (Hilkhot Avadim 3:4) that anyone who has a wife and children, and even if the wife is forbidden by negative prohibitions, the master may give her to him, etc. And this is the verse's saying <b>אִם בַּעַל אִשָּׁה הוּא</b> — meaning, it is not required that his wife be fitting for him with regard to what I prohibited to you in the nearby verse ״אִם בְּגַפּוֹ״ (that his master may not give him), but with regard to food, <b>וְיָצְאָה אִשְׁתּוֹ</b> — meaning, one who is fitting for him is the one for whom the master is obligated to provide food." "<b>Further,</b> it is explained according to the statement of our Sages of blessed memory (Mekhilta), and this is their language: One might think he is obligated to provide food for his betrothed (arusah) or his yavam-bound widow (shomeret yavam)? Scripture says ״אִשְׁתּוֹ״ (his wife) — to exclude the yavam-bound widow; ״עִמּוֹ״ (with him) — to exclude his betrothed. Now, a slave who had a yavam-bound widow or a betrothed woman may be given a Canaanite maidservant, and this is the verse's saying <b>אִם בַּעַל אִשָּׁה הוּא</b> — meaning without condition: even if she is a betrothed woman or a yavam-bound widow, he has left the category of בְּגַפּוֹ under which I prohibited to you the giving of a maidservant. And according to the words of the Talmud (Kiddushin 20a) which derived the children as well, the same ruling would also apply in a case where he had children from another wife and she died and he betrothed a woman — the giving of a maidservant is permitted." "<b>If he came alone, he shall go out alone; if he is married.</b> [The verse] says it with a vav, for since HaShem commanded regarding a wife's food, it is fitting to say a vav — for besides that he is obligated in her food, also etc."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:31:46.767241849Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}